  "response_text": "Always choosing the highest-priority thread can lead to starvation\u2014lower-priority threads may never get a chance to run if a high-priority thread is always ready. This breaks fairness and defeats the purpose of cooperative multitasking.\\nAdditionally, this makes the system less predictable. Round-robin ensures each thread gets CPU time, but a strict priority scheduler can get stuck running a few high-priority threads endlessly. It also adds complexity: the scheduler must scan all threads for the highest priority every time, which can be inefficient compared to simply rotating through them.\\nA better approach is priority aging or priority with round-robin fallback, which keeps the system responsive and fair.\\n",
